      </style></head><body><div id="banners"><div id="publish-banner"><span id="publish-banner-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" height="24px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24px"><path d="M11 7h2v2h-2zm0 4h2v6h-2zm1-9C6.48 2 2 6.48 2 12s4.48 10 10 10 10-4.48 10-10S17.52 2 12 2zm0 18c-4.41 0-8-3.59-8-8s3.59-8 8-8 8 3.59 8 8-3.59 8-8 8z"/></svg></span><div id="publish-banner-text">Opublikowano za pomocą Dokumentów Google</div><div id="publish-banner-buttons"><span><a href="https://drive.google.com/abuse?id=AKkXjowsUY61WJyRJWMocetRnKFTHp2e0kuHhJaN9-tdx--guBvSlnxayqUqhEDLvGxugt4C2EZoBq_1fEuKwc0:0&amp;docurl=https://docs.google.com/document/d/e/2PACX-1vTY8v80kOcpNVp1zLWAWKF9G9eoKAdpPVDooSu0vfaG8SwD2g8awTzd-WBHq2UsezZ8LuTgwETYIZ4k/pub?output%3Dtxt">Zgłoś nadużycie</a></span><span><a target="_blank" title="Learn more" href="https://support.google.com/docs/answer/183965">Więcej informacji</a></span></div></div><div id="title-banner"><div id="title">Butoh and Contact Improvisation</div><div id="interval"><span>Automatyczne aktualizacje co 5 min</span></div></div></div><div id="contents"><style type="text/css">ol{margin:0;padding:0}table td,table th{padding:0}.c3{margin-left:48pt;padding-top:0pt;text-indent:-24pt;padding-bottom:0pt;line-height:1.15;orphans:2;widows:2;text-align:left}.c0{color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:12pt;font-family:"Arial";font-style:normal}.c8{color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:"Arial";font-style:normal}.c5{padding-top:0pt;padding-bottom:0pt;line-height:1.15;orphans:2;widows:2;text-align:left}.c1{padding-top:0pt;padding-bottom:0pt;line-height:1.15;orphans:2;widows:2;text-align:justify}.c7{background-color:#ffffff;max-width:595.4pt;padding:0pt 0pt 0pt 0pt}.c2{font-size:12pt;font-style:italic}.c4{font-size:12pt}.c6{height:11pt}.title{padding-top:0pt;color:#000000;font-size:26pt;padding-bottom:3pt;font-family:"Arial";line-height:1.15;page-break-after:avoid;orphans:2;widows:2;text-align:left}.subtitle{padding-top:0pt;color:#666666;font-size:15pt;padding-bottom:16pt;font-family:"Arial";line-height:1.15;page-break-after:avoid;orphans:2;widows:2;text-align:left}li{color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:"Arial"}p{margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:"Arial"}h1{padding-top:20pt;color:#000000;font-size:20pt;padding-bottom:6pt;font-family:"Arial";line-height:1.15;page-break-after:avoid;orphans:2;widows:2;text-align:left}h2{padding-top:18pt;color:#000000;font-size:16pt;padding-bottom:6pt;font-family:"Arial";line-height:1.15;page-break-after:avoid;orphans:2;widows:2;text-align:left}h3{padding-top:16pt;color:#434343;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:4pt;font-family:"Arial";line-height:1.15;page-break-after:avoid;orphans:2;widows:2;text-align:left}h4{padding-top:14pt;color:#666666;font-size:12pt;padding-bottom:4pt;font-family:"Arial";line-height:1.15;page-break-after:avoid;orphans:2;widows:2;text-align:left}h5{padding-top:12pt;color:#666666;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:4pt;font-family:"Arial";line-height:1.15;page-break-after:avoid;orphans:2;widows:2;text-align:left}h6{padding-top:12pt;color:#666666;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:4pt;font-family:"Arial";line-height:1.15;page-break-after:avoid;font-style:italic;orphans:2;widows:2;text-align:left}</style><div class="c7 doc-content"><p class="c1"><span class="c4">I claim that despite clear differences in style, approach, and technique, in both Butoh and Contact Improvisation, mainstream concepts of beauty, gender, social roles, neatness, disability, among others, are deconstructed and redefined. I will firstly introduce the slightly older </span><span class="c2">Butoh</span><span class="c4"> dance, its historical context and main characteristics. Secondly, I will present </span><span class="c2">Contact Improvisation</span><span class="c0"> and the elements that distinguish it from Butoh. Finally, I will compare the two dances and draw conclusions about the elements that differentiate them as well as those they have in common, which I claim that reconstruct and redefine mainstream values in the global society.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c4">Butoh, originated in post Second World War Japan in 1959 through the collaboration of Tatsumi Hijikata and Kazuo Ohno. They originally named it the dance of darkness or</span><span class="c2"> ankoku butoh</span><span class="c0"> (暗黒舞踏) in Japanese, as it strived to express the hidden, the denied, the tabus, the ugliness, in a context where ballet, as an stylized form, was the dominant influence. This association with the social taboos, made Butoh an object of harsh moral criticism that eventually led to the exile of its creator Hijikata from the Association for Contemporary Performing Arts, particularly after a provocative performance involving rape and a chicken (Moore, 2006).</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">In the context of the post Second World War Japan, with two atomic bombs and the imposition of western culture in virtually every aspect of the Japanese society, namely law, the economy, fashion, technology, Arts, etc. it seems reasonable that Butoh would appear as a voice of rebellion against all that. However, the Japanese society of that time was not ready to radically replace their comfortably installed set of moral values with those proposed by Butoh, and thus the dance was confined to marginality.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Contact Improvisation, on the other hand, originated in 1972 when Steve Paxton led a group of dancers to perform a study of how touch can function as a means of communication. The result was “a non-rationalized, intuitive movement leading to unforeseen phrasing, positions and gambits.” (Kaltenbrunner i Procyk 2004, 23; Chien i Yi 2014). The new dance aimed at breaking the cultural taboo of touch, so strongly connected with specific social behaviors. To refrain from talking and laughter was necessary to allow the real feelings of fear, arousal or indignance to emerge as a result of the physical phenomena, namely the touch, weight, gravity, momentum, among others (ibid). The strong anchorage between touch and social meaning was starting to be deconstructed as the touching experiences by the dancers were not required to be dealt with on a social level (ibid). Breaking away from the connection between touch and its socially constructed meaning questioned the core building block through which society is symbolically constituted.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Despite the fact that both Contact Improvisation and Butoh question the cultural establishment’s core values and openly focus on deconstructing social taboos, they seem as radically different approaches to dance. Butoh dancers paint their skin white, to emphasize their emotional expressions, which was also perceived as rebellious act within the Japanese postwar context (Freleigh 1959, 13:46). Butoh dancers’ often frightening appearance and movements contrast with the natural flow and rhythmic playfulness displayed by Contact Improvisation dancers.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Technically, Contact Improvisation is considered as a mixture between contemporary dance, meditation and martial arts. Of particular importance is the Japanese marcial arts Aikido, which teaches that the energy of an offensive movement can be seized as momentum for another. The result is a harmonic flow of movements with rhythmic interactions that clearly contrasts with Butoh’s pseudo epileptic, cathartic body expression. While in Butoh, dancers are often practically naked, exhibiting their white-painted skin, CI dancers are informally dressed in random simple clothes to allow freedom of movement. In Butoh, the expression of feelings take priority over the interaction with the physical conditions, like in CI. Despite of this, the cause of movement in Butoh are the physical conditions. Butoh dancers present themselves as objects rather than subjects of dance, visibly affected by the changing physical conditions, not only arising from the natural environment, but also by the inner personal environment.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">CI dancers on the other hand, interact with one another after having found a certain inner peace through warming-up, stretching, relaxing and meditating techniques. CI’s nature is inherently based on interaction with other dancers, unlike Butoh’s which often tends to solo dancer formats. Even when the format of the Butoh performance involves other dancers, they do not appear to interact in a human-like manner, at most they appear to understand they belong to the same species.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Redefining Humanity</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Over years, anthropologists have been debating over the question of what it means to be human. The identification of Human Universals, as a set of behaviors with transcultural validity oppose to Cultural Relativism’s theory whereby no universal law can be applied to a human behavior, as this fully depends on the cultural background where it originated. Butoh and CI are two examples of dance in which the mainstream values of Humanity are questioned.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Ballet and the moral value of beauty</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c4">In Butoh, the mundane, the ugly, the marginated are not ignored but on the contrary, emphasized. As we mentioned before, Butoh was born out of a reaction against the western imposition of moral values, among which was the western concept of “beauty”, that came along with expressions such as Ballet. Dancers in Ballet, always hold an upright torso position, even when they lie on the ground. The chest is open and pointe feet support the whole weight of the body, symbolizing the need to stay as close as possible from the holy heavens.  It is easy to guess the origin of the upright position in the </span><span class="c2">Homo erectus</span><span class="c0"> species which was the first human ancestor to walk in this manner, shaping the mainstream definition of man, as differentiated from other apes. In Butoh, the body’s center of gravity stays closer to the ground, often with bended knees or in fetal position when lying on the ground. There is no attempt to decorate the movement, neither to make it smoother. There is, however, an inner strength that makes the dancer move with great sensitivity, reacting to every aspect of their environment. The facial expressions in Butoh are not overlooked in this process, and stay in total syntony with the dancers’ body movements, expressing a variety of emotions that are not easily connected with those used in social interaction.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">CI’s concept of beauty is related to the smoothness and richness of the touching interaction, but most importantly, on the authenticity of the dancer’s diverse expression. Brozas Polo and Maria Paz (2013) describe this as “a utopian pedagogical principle that allows and power the diversity of the bodies who dance: from the point of view of the skill, ability or disability, and also on the age and gender.” Unlike Ballet, where every movement is calculated, in CI, the spontaneous fall is encouraged and celebrated as a beautiful and natural outcome of a previous movement. Moreover, ﻿in 1986, Anne Kilcoyne y Steve Paxton organized in collaboration with the ﻿Royal National Institute for the Blind in Torquay’s Manor House, a series of annual courses, where people with visual disability participated, together with dancers from the Royal Institute and interested professors (ibid). Through these experiences, CI was putting forward a new aesthetic where the disabled could also be beautiful.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Democratizing the experience of dance</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">One of the characteristic of Butoh and CI is its inclusiveness. Most dances require to spend several years of training at specialized academies to build up a specific technique as well in many dances such as Ballet, a specific physique allowing the dancers to perform. In contrast, Butoh and CI are accessible for anybody regardless of physique, age, gender, or ability. In Butoh, an older dancer does not pretend to appear younger than he is. An example is Daisuke Yoshimoto, who is still an active Butoh dancer over the age of 70. Wearing a long white hair and beard, he dances half naked, usually in concentrated slow movements that direct the attention to his inner emotions, through his older physicality.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Just as men, women, young and old, performing solo or group dances, usually with their practically naked bodies painted in white, search for a truthful release of their inner self. A search that is shared by CI, ﻿reflected in the openness to welcome unexperienced dancers. In 1988, Danny Lepkoff wrote an article in Contact Quarterly under the title &quot;Questions not to ask&quot;. One of these questions was: “Why complete beginners are so beautiful to watch, and experienced Contact Improvisers often seem so boring?” (Kaltenbrunner i Procyk 2004, 27). Amateur dancers are believed to add value by bringing their own previous experience in whatever field they developed, to the dance. This inclusiveness is based on the idea that everyone has developed a unique sense motion, shaped by their life experiences. Instead of denying or getting rid of it, CI attempts to seize it as a source of expressive richness. In Butoh too, Daisuke Yoshimoto used to point out that what a dancer can perform with his body and soul is the very experience that has marked them in their past. Thus, Butoh performers should look around very attentively wherever they go, because the sources of inspiration may be anywhere for the attentive ones.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Improvisation, attentiveness and Composition</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Another common feature of both Butoh and CI is its present-tense liveliness commonly attributed to a somewhat improvised nature. Although both dances are often regarded as free experimental improvisation around a given theme based on a set of trade-mark techniques identifying each of the dances, that is not accurate. In fact Hijikata rarely improvised even from his beginnings (although presentations such as the wind- sprints or cake- eating of Rose- colored Dance were not entirely planned out) (Baird 2012, 211). It is a common misconception to associate an intrinsically spontaneous or improvised nature to contemporary arts in general. In both Butoh and CI there is indeed a well-defined structure but unlike Ballet or other dances, it is mainly correlated to the following factors: the environment, the theme, the characters, and their impulses or searches. A good analogy to compare contemporary dances such as Butoh and CI with Ballet or more structured dances would be Documentary Cinema versus Fictional Cinema. In the latter, the script, the stage, the actors’ lines, the montage is all strictly defined leaving no room for improvisation. The former, on the other hand, is eager to present its conjunctural nature. In other words, dancers of Butoh and CI are extremely attentive and reactive to the elements that surround them to convey their message.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Often, the only presence of a Butoh dancer is in itself the main focus of the act’s attention. This has a to do with traditional Japanese performing arts such as Kabuki, where the performer’s gestures even the small ones, are enough to personify the characters’ attitudes or social stand and attract to them the audience’s full attention. In CI, changes in the movement’s rhythm, the demand-response dynamics, and particularly the characteristic search for surprise, play a significant role in the general mood of the CI performance. These elements conform a structure common in both dances whereby the environmental factors (symbolic or not) affecting the dancers and their capacity to make them seem real and vivid to the audience, lie on the core (Kaltenbrunner i Procyk 2004, 67). The resulting effect is a lively and moment-aware performance that can keep the audience’s full attention regardless of a full understanding of its script.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Going further than dance: Sexuality and Social behavior</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Making the performance seem as natural as an improvisation, has social side-effects. Due to its lively, moment-oriented nature, the question of where to draw a line between the dance and the social reality that it questions and deconstructs is particularly relevant for both Butoh and CI. We mentioned earlier that ﻿for Paxton, CI provided a platform “to go into areas that invoked things on a social nature but without being obliged to deal with them on a social level” (Kaltenbrunner i Procyk 2004, 23). Understanding society as a building made up of a set of social behaviors with social meaning attached to them, are dancers really free to break away from those meanings, leaving them to the performance only? And if so, but they choose not to, what are the implications of those social effects to create social reality? Are Butoh and CI dances contributing to revendicate Art’s capacity to shape society, against the capitalist tendency to subjugate it to consumable entertainment?</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">In many cultures, touch is considered taboo, and in every culture, it is loaded with social meaning. At HMP Dovegate, an English maximum security prison, long-term inmates volunteered to participate in two-weeks dance workshops, led by the contemporary dance company Motionhouse Dance Theatre. The participants were stimulated to discover their own modes of executing the movements, rather than just mimicking how the instructor had completed them. In discovering their own pathways through movement, the emphasis was placed on appreciating participants&#39; creativity and promoting movement&#39;s flow and energy, rather than on producing dance that is technically impeccable (Houston 2009, 98). That is how these CI workshops were able to teach the maximum security prisoners how to relate to others in a non-sexual and non-confrontational manner, something difficult to do within a prison.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Sexuality, understood as the way people experience and express themselves sexually (Akstulewicz 2015; Wilson 2019), has been impacted by Butoh and CI. As I mentioned before, Butoh was born from a reaction to the western imposition of values, among which was the idea of beauty, gender roles, morality, taboos, among others. Ballet, as the main source of these concepts, would present a perfect body as correspondent with that of a western person, a specific muscular, thin and slender type. Men and women would behave in movement in accordance with specific western concepts of masculinity and femininity, correspondently.   Soon Hijikata realized that neither the Japanese body type nor its need for expressing a more authentic way of movement or dance theme would be accepted in the Japanese dance circles. With denied bodies and movements, sexuality would be strongly repressed, and the attempts to express a Japanese temperament, were looked down upon. Butoh, thus came as a way to express the unwanted, the repressed, but also, as a search for honesty revendicating the authenticity of the local. Naked women and men would not try to replicate the sexual meanings attributed to their body parts as imposed by Ballet, and would rather tend to connect the whole body movement to their expression of feeling as the guiding drive, despite of the weirdness of its movement from a western perspective. Sexuality would simply be expressed differently, often in the form of a social protest, such as the early presentations by Hijikata on the theme of homosexuality, or social violence against men and women, and their reaction to it (Baird 2012, 21).</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">CI, on the other hand, would not present itself as a direct sexual protest but rather as a reform of sexual dynamics, namely gender roles, concepts of beauty and of course sexual taboos. The last, in terms of how to reconnect with touch, opening its horizons beyond its strong association with sexuality, without denying it, but giving space to other feelings, such as tenderness, playfulness, symbolic theatrical team-work creation, brotherhood, vulnerability, among many others. Gender roles would create situations in which a smaller-sized woman could lift a man, something impossible in expressions such as Ballet. </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Conclusion</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0">Despite the several differences and similitudes between Butoh and CI, many of which I could not refer as I did not have space to touch upon, such as the inside/outside space, or pair/group dynamics differing in Butoh and CI, I believe I could make the point of presenting a brief analysis of certain key factors that make both dances, sources of social change. It is an aspiration to see artistic expressions contribute to the opening of our perspectives regarding our relations among ourselves and towards our environment. Despite their totally different origins, responding to different original intentions, the continuous evolution, readaptation and interpretation of Butoh and CI by the different schools, chorographers and dancers, according to their views, understanding and need for expression is, I believe, what makes their value. In the current modern context, were we have walked away from a spiritual connection with the body, I believe that Arts presents a great hope to renew our flawed modus operandi, and replenish them with vitality, uniting theory with practice.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c4">Akstulewicz, Frank. 2015. „A global perspective”. </span><span class="c2">Nuclear Plant Journal</span><span class="c0">. https://books.google.com/books/about/Sociology_A_Global_Perspective.html?hl=pl&amp;id=Au4bCgAAQBAJ.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c4">Baird, Bruce. 2012. </span><span class="c2">Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh</span><span class="c4">. </span><span class="c2">Hijikata Tatsumi and Butoh</span><span class="c0">. https://doi.org/10.1057/9781137012623.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c4">Brozas Polo, Maria Paz. 2013. „La accesibilidad en la danza contact improvisation”. </span><span class="c2">Arte y Movimiento</span><span class="c0">, 33–44.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c4">Chien, Wen-Yin, i Chin-Chun Yi. 2014. „Marital Power Structure in Two Chinese Societies: Measurement and Mechanisms”. </span><span class="c2">Journal of Comparative Family Studies</span><span class="c0"> 45 (63): 93--+. http://apps.webofknowledge.com.proxy2.athensams.net/full_record.do?product=UA&amp;search_mode=GeneralSearch&amp;qid=6&amp;SID=S2hxfYndq8mHvFe24eE&amp;page=1&amp;doc=1.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c4">Freleigh, Sondra Horton. 1959. </span><span class="c2">Dancing into Darkness: Butoh, Zen and Japan</span><span class="c4">. </span><span class="c2">Nucl. Phys.</span><span class="c0"> T. 13.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c4">Houston, Sara. 2009. „The touch ‘taboo’ and the art of contact: an exploration of Contact Improvisation for prisoners”. </span><span class="c2">Research in Dance Education</span><span class="c0"> 10 (2): 97–113. https://doi.org/10.1080/14647890903019432.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c4">Kaltenbrunner, Thomas., i Nick. Procyk. 2004. </span><span class="c2">Contact improvisation : moving, dancing, interaction : with an introduction to new dance</span><span class="c0">. USA: Meyer und Meyer.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c4">Wilson, Edward O. 2019. „Sex and Society”. W </span><span class="c2">Sociobiology</span><span class="c0">, 314–35. https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvjnrttd.17.</span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c1"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c5"><span class="c0"> </span></p><p class="c5 c6"><span class="c0"></span></p><p class="c5 c6"><span class="c8"></span></p></div></div><script nonce="W8y6_9iw-FjXaBqaIQQGvA">function _F_toggles_initialize(a){(typeof globalThis!=="undefined"?globalThis:typeof self!=="undefined"?self:this)._F_toggles=a||[]}_F_toggles_initialize([]);
